A||||14||ponya||Brake master cylinder||||5||
Z||000000||ponya||12-21-2000||07:06 PM||||Hi all, still working on that mushy brake problem. I have a question in regards to MC's. I bought the one that is in the car now from Automotive discount for $20.00. I priced one at NAPA automotive and it was $60.00 for a rebuilt and about $100.00 for a new one. Pontiac wanted if I remember about $120.00.<BR>I'm basically thinking about the one from Auto discount and the one from NAPA. Is there a signifigant difference in quality between the two? Should I have gone for the $60.00 one from NAPA. My son seems to think that they are both the same. Thought I would put it out to you guys to see what you think. Any imput greatly appreciated. Might even help me solve the mushy brake problem!   Thanks,  Bob||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||12-21-2000||11:20 PM||||Don't have any experience with NAPA master cylinders - but, not knowing anything else, I would probably choose the $60 rebuilt NAPA myself, <B>if</B> NAPA offers a lifetime warranty.  Otherwise, I'd probably choose the $120 Pontiac unit.<P>Reasons: There braking system is split, so if the M/C fails, you still have 1/2 the brakes; I could live with that until I put in the replacement (under lifetime warranty).  $60 vs. $120 is a significant savings, and changing out the M/C isn't difficult.  If there's no warranty, I wouldn't do it, though.<P>The $120 Pontiac replacement is worth it (brakes are kind of important!) if you don't want to mess with it ever again (well, for a long time, anyway).  IMO, $100 for the NAPA "new" unit is too close to the $120 Pontiac M/C to mess with, unless you have reason to believe the NAPA "new" is better than the Pontiac.  <P>All I know is, I've used rebuilt brake parts (not the M/C, though) on my cars, without problems.  Also, NAPA generally seems to carry good quality parts. <P>Just my humble opinion.  Hopefully, some of the other members have some real data to go on.        ||||reg||2
Z||000002||theogre||12-22-2000||11:02 AM||||I don't know how much you know about master cyls. There are 2 pistons in there. If the seal is bad on the inner piston you could have these kinds of problems. <P>The inner piston usually has air trapped in it. if the seals are bad the result is the same as a bad bleeding job. You'll never get it to work right if those seals are hosed.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||12-22-2000||11:19 AM||||I have heard that the M/C for a full sized Blazer (1994 is the year I think) Is a bolt-on replacement. From what I understand there are 2 advantages. Less expensive than the Fiero one and has a bigger bore for better braking. If you plan on replacing it again I would look into it. <BR>As for quality, I have bought parts from NAPA and others. Although some places are less expensive than NAPA, I have never had a problem with the quality of NAPA parts. Some of the cheaper parts purchased from other stores were also cheaper in quality. <P>Also what was the brand of M/C you bought? I know someone who bought a GBC Industries M/C for his clutch. It didn't work right. Upon checking it out he found that they rebuilt a GM unit and honed out the cylinder a little too much.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 12-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||12-22-2000||01:00 PM||||I probably wouldn't use the Blazer M/C unless you have bigger caliper pistons than stock (i.e. Grand Am brakes).<P>I would use caution about buying a rebuilt M/C, epecially a cheapie no-name brand, which is what it sounds like you did. I personally would not buy one from Pontiac either... unless you like spending two as much as it's worth.<P>My personal opinion, supported by experience, would be to buy a new Bendix M/C. You can have most major auto parts stores order them if they are not in stock. NAPA usually carries good quality parts, but you can usually find the same parts cheaper at other places. Shop around. Good luck.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 12-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||william_thorniii||12-22-2000||01:07 PM||||Orief,<P>Bigger doesn't necessarily mean better in master cylinders.  Changing the master cyl. diameter will decrease the amount of pressure generated in the fluid for the same force input.  Typically around 400-500 psi is lost with a .1" increase in diameter.  I guess one would have to determine the differences in pressure between the two systems and calculate back to clamp pressure at the calipers to determine if this swap is detrimental or not.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000006||Oreif||12-22-2000||02:34 PM||||WTIII, In an enclosed hydralic system I would assume that more pressure would be generated with same travel. (I say assume since I am not an expert on hydralics) As an example, If I had a cylinder .5" long and .5" in diameter that fed a .3" hose and measured the pressure at the output as 10lbs. Then I hook up a .5" long by .75" diameter cylinder to the same hose, It would require less travel to exert 10lbs at the output. (More fluid is pushed with same distance). So same travel would increase pressure since more fluid would be pushed. As for input pressure (brake pedal) I would assume less pressure would be required since the applied pressure would be even across the face of the piston but move more fluid by volume.<BR>I think that is right from what I remember of my fluid dynamics class many moons ago. I do know that air systems are opposite since air is a compressable gas. (More air/larger bore requires more pressure to compress since the compession is spread evenly across the air molecules)<BR>If this is incorrect, let me know.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 12-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phil||12-22-2000||03:33 PM||||Volume aside for the moment, in brakes you are concerned with caliper clamp pressure. Assume you want 100 lb clamp pressure and you have a 1 sq.inch caliper piston this means you have to have 100 psi line pressure. Now say your MC  has a 2 sq. in piston, you now need 200 lbs applied to the MC to get 100 psi line pressure.  Force applied/piston area = p.s.i. In conclusion the larger the MC piston the more pedal effort required to generate the same clamp force at the caliper. <BR>The above was brought to you by the Randolph School of retired Chem engineers<p>[This message has been edited by Phil (edited 12-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Oreif||12-22-2000||03:40 PM||||AHH I see, So If the larger M/C was used, That means you need to apply more pressure but pedal travel would be less. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Phil||12-22-2000||04:19 PM||||Bingo!!!||||reg||1
Z||000010||william_thorniii||12-22-2000||04:51 PM||||Phil,<P>How do I register for class in the Randolph School of Retired Chem. Engineers?  I bet there would be some good classes there.  <P>Orief,<P>You are beginning to see the light.  First of all for this comparison, we should neglect variations in pedal pressure.  Assume the same force is input and the pedal is of the same ratio.  This way we can restrict this comparison to one variable factor.  <P>Typically you want to use the smallest mc possible for the amount of brake fluid you need to push.  Also remember that the first amount of pedal travel is required to remove compliance from the system (rubber expansion, etc.).  <P>To calculate the clamp pressure that Phil refers to you do the following:  Determine the pressure output from the mc to each line.  We will assume that to be 500 #/in^2.  If the caliper has 4 1.75" (Wilwood Dynalite II) we can assume that each piston receives the same amount of fluid and therefore each piston exerts the same force on the pad.  So the area of the piston is pi*r^2 and is 2.405 in^2 and the clamp load from each piston would be (2.405in^2*500 #/in^2) 1202.64# and total caliper clamp load would be 4810.56#.  <P>Keep those pencils going... cause we are not done yet.  Then you can calculate the clamp load on the rotor from the pad.  This is simply done by multipling the clamp force by the coefficient of friction of the pad.  A good assumption is .45.  So no we take 4810.56#*.45*2 = 4329.51#.  ****I don't quite understand the multiplying by two, it was in my notes though.****<P>So you can see that by both increasing the piston diameter and coefficient of friction of the brake pad that clamp pressure is increased.  When you increase the piston diameter you need to increase the mc though.  Therein lies the advantage of high performance brake pads.  <P>Assuming we are using a 12.19" Wilwood rotor, we find a distance of approximately 10.5" between the centerline of the rotor and the caliper pressure point.  This is not exact.  It is assumed that all pressure is located in the center of the rotor braking surfce.  So now the torque applied on the center of the rotor is 4329.51# * 10.5in / 12in/ft=3788.32ft-#  This is the force that must be exceeded by tire grip to stop a car.  <P>I don't know where I was going with this... but I already had my notes out and thought some of you might want to know the calculations.  From here you can calculate stopping distance,etc.  The most important calculation I did not address... the limits of tire adhesion.  Those notes are missing from my folder.  I will try to find them over the weekend if anyone cares.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000011||StuGood||12-22-2000||05:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by william_thorniii:<BR><B>...So no we take 4810.56#*.45*2 = 4329.51#.  ****I don't quite understand the multiplying by two, it was in my notes though.****...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Think you multiply by 2, because there's an equal but opposite reaction to the 4810 lbf, on the other side of the rotor.  In other words, it's the reaction from other (outboard) brake pad.<P>So generally speaking... higher stomping force, smaller m/c piston dia., bigger caliper piston dia., higher coeff. of friction...  all these result in more stopping force.  Until the wheels lock up, of course  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000012||Phil||12-22-2000||07:19 PM|||| Just to keep this going. Coef. of friction  changes when you lock the wheels- goes from dynamic friction to static in relation to the brake pads and rotors but the tires go from static to dynamic with relation to the pavement. Static being higher than dynamic. WOW now this is high power stuff. Get your tution in for the second semester.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>ENOUGH ENOUGH||||reg||1
Z||000013||StuGood||12-22-2000||07:39 PM||||Gee, WT III must be vying for my "Mr. Windy" title   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Once we get started talking about brakes, there's just no stopping us, eh   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">?<P>So...  square that quantity, carry the "2," multiply by pi, sum all of those terms, take the square root, convert millimeters to inches, and the answer is:<P>Batboy says go for the Bendix M/C.  It worked for him  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">    <P><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 12-22-2000).]||||reg||12
Z||000014||Indiana_resto_guy||12-22-2000||07:55 PM||||BOY!<BR>After reading this thread, I feel like I just got out of trig class! (again!) LOL||||reg||1
